      "content": "You are correct that a p-value below 0.05 theoretically means the effect is statistically significant, but a 95% confidence interval that includes zero logically contradicts that conclusion because it directly implies the result is not significant. By definition, if you construct a 95% confidence interval for a null hypothesis test where the null value (usually zero) falls inside the range, the p-value must be greater than 0.05, creating a mathematical impossibility if your numbers are simultaneously accurate. You should verify whether your p-value of 0.042 might actually be for a one-tailed test while the interval represents a two-tailed result, or if there is a calculation discrepancy that needs correction.",
