      "content": "No, you are correct that a one-tailed p-value of 0.042 often pairs with a two-tailed 95% confidence interval in social science software, because the one-tailed significance level corresponds to the two-tailed alpha of 0.10. This isn't a hidden error in the data, but rather a convention where the p-value and confidence interval are calculated using different underlying assumptions without always being explicitly marked for you. The \"messiness\" you feel comes from statistical packages sometimes defaulting to one-tailed tests for specific hypotheses while displaying two-tailed intervals unless you carefully specify otherwise. As long as you clearly state your testing framework in your thesis, the math remains consistent, but reader clarity often requires you to explicitly define why you chose a one-tailed test to avoid confusion.",
